## Main audit conclusion\
\
The largest polish opportunity is restraint.\
\
The site repeatedly communicates the same claims before visitors reach the work. The hero currently contains:\
\
- Availability badge\
- Large positioning headline\
- Long summary\
- Technology list\
- Four-tab terminal\
- Career-status card\
- Four-item proof strip\
\
Several of these repeat the same messages:\
\
- 7+ years\
- Full SDLC\
- Java/Spring\
- Modernization\
- Secure systems\
- Scalable systems\
- Production-grade systems\
- Availability\
\
Observed homepage repetition:\
\
- \'93full SDLC\'94: 8 occurrences\
- \'93production\'94: 20 occurrences\
- \'93secure\'94: 10 occurrences\
- \'93scalable\'94: 6 occurrences\
- \'93AI-assisted\'94: 2 occurrences\
\
The hero should still be expressive, but every component should have a distinct purpose.\
\
Suggested division of responsibilities:\
\
- Headline: clear professional positioning\
- Summary: specialization and value\
- Terminal: personality or technical evidence\
- Proof strip: concrete scale and outcomes\
\
The terminal, status card, and proof strip currently overlap substantially.\
\
## Prioritized polish recommendations\
\
### 1. Reduce opening-page repetition\
\
Highest priority.\
\
Trim or consolidate duplicated information across:\
\
- Hero summary\
- Terminal Overview\
- Terminal Qualifications\
- Career-status card\
- Proof strip\
- Technology list\
\
The objective is not necessarily to remove the terminal. It is the site\'92s most distinctive visual element. The objective is to make it carry information that the surrounding hero does not already state.\
\
A shorter path from the opening headline to Selected Work would make the portfolio feel more confident and recruiter-friendly.\
\
### 2. Sharpen the positioning statement\
\
Current hero:\
\
> Senior Software Engineer Building Intelligent, Scalable Systems.\
\
This sounds polished but generic. The site\'92s actual differentiators are more specific:\
\
- Java and Spring modernization\
- Public-sector engineering\
- MFA and secure access control\
- Nationwide reporting workflows\
- Full production ownership\
- Legacy-to-maintainable-system transitions\
\
Bring one or two differentiators into the positioning rather than relying mainly on \'93intelligent\'94 and \'93scalable.\'94\
\
Also reconcile the title hierarchy:\
\
- Hero: \'93Senior Software Engineer\'94\
- Terminal: \'93Software Engineer\'94\
- About portrait: \'93Software Engineer\'94\
- Latest role: \'93Software Engineer II\'94\
- Metadata: \'93Software Engineer\'94\
\
This does not mean \'93Senior\'94 is necessarily incorrect. The wording should simply be intentional and consistent enough that recruiters do not pause over it.\
\
### 3. Replace broad claims with evidence\
\
The site uses many quality adjectives:\
\
- Secure\
- Scalable\
- Production-grade\
- Credible\
- Accessible\
- Maintainable\
- Reliable\
\
These are strongest when supported with concrete information.\
\
Where confidentiality permits, polish the existing content with:\
\
- Number of jurisdictions\
- Number or scale of workflows\
- User or organizational reach\
- Number of applications modernized\
- Specific before/after technical changes\
- Performance results\
- Accessibility audit results\
- Deployment or release ownership\
- Exact personal responsibility versus team responsibility\
\
\'93Nationwide reporting\'94 is currently the most concrete scale signal and deserves greater prominence.\
